From 7b245151329a3b19a17bca10718eb1494ed63cfc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: vbardales <virginie.bardales@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 22:37:34 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] fix(dashboard): do not translate while the game has no
 active language

DashboardStrings.Translate calls CanTranslate, which dereferences
LanguageDatabase.activeLanguage. That field is null for as long as the game is
reloading its language data: SelectLanguage does not finish inside the call, and
every frame in between has no active language at all - the log fills with "No
active language! Cannot translate from key" while it lasts.

A snapshot published in that window therefore throws NullReferenceException out
of DashboardStrings.BuildJson, through RunnerSnapshot.Build and PublishSnapshot,
and takes the run with it: the autorun stops with exitReason
"infrastructure-error" and exit code 2, the scenario that happened to be running
is reported as failing with nothing but "Object reference not set to an instance
of an object", and the game is then left unable to quit - no further log output,
process alive.

Any suite with a scenario that switches language hits this. Observed on
2026-09-20 in a headless Linux run (RimWorld 1.6.4871, Pickle 4.8.0): a scenario
calling LanguageDatabase.SelectLanguage lost the run and hung the game, and the
step-level failure message named a step that had nothing to do with it.

Guarding Translate is enough, and it degrades the way the method already does
for a missing key: the label comes back empty and the browser decides. A
dashboard label is never worth a run.

AI-assisted: Claude Code

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 Source/Pickle/Web/DashboardStrings.cs | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/Source/Pickle/Web/DashboardStrings.cs b/Source/Pickle/Web/DashboardStrings.cs
index 53d1ab6..16c1ac9 100644
--- a/Source/Pickle/Web/DashboardStrings.cs
+++ b/Source/Pickle/Web/DashboardStrings.cs
@@ -69,8 +69,15 @@ public static class DashboardStrings {
 
   // A key with no entry translates to the key itself, which would read as
   // "Pickle_RunAll" on a button. Fall back to nothing and let the browser decide.
+  //
+  // There may also be no language at all. SelectLanguage does not finish inside the call: the
+  // game reloads the language data and only then points activeLanguage at it, and every frame in
+  // between has none. CanTranslate dereferences it, so a snapshot published in that window throws
+  // NullReferenceException - out of PublishSnapshot, out of the runner, and the whole run dies
+  // with exitReason "infrastructure-error" while the game is left unable to quit. A scenario that
+  // switches language is enough to hit it. A dashboard label is never worth a run.
   private static string Translate(string key) {
-    if (!key.CanTranslate()) {
+    if (LanguageDatabase.activeLanguage == null || !key.CanTranslate()) {
       return string.Empty;
     }
 
